WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.540
Batman is arguably the most iconic comic book
hero of all time.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.740
But what's his connection with a senator from
Vermont?

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.789
Who are the real-life Batmen?

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:11.690
And who really came up with the character
in the first place?

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:16.480
Keep watching to find out more about everyone's
favorite Caped Crusader.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.230
Most people are aware of the impact that Bob
Kane had on the creation of the World's Greatest

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:24.260
Detective — but he wasn't the only person
who can put his name to Batman.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.980
Indeed, when Bill Finger earned a credit as
the character's co-creator in 2015, it marked

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.890
the end of a long struggle for recognition
for this little-known comic book writer.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:37.010
In recent years, it has become widely accepted
that Finger was responsible for many of the

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.120
innovations that had previously been credited
to Kane.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.920
This included the idea to turn Kane's red-shirted
early concept for Batman into the design that

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.760
ultimately became the Dark Knight's first
costume.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.559
Not only that, but Finger also conceived,
co-created, or co-developed both Alfred and

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.070
Commissioner Gordon, as well as Catwoman,
the Joker, and even Robin.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.730
The latter two were also heavily influenced
by Jerry Robinson, who designed both characters

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.390
and who, according to him, actually came up
with the Joker in the first place.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:09.610
Robinson also claims that he gave the Boy
Wonder his superhero name.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.280
Of course, none of this is to say that Bob
Kane didn't have a hand in crafting Batman's

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.880
world — but his contributions are as overstated
as Finger and Robinson's are overlooked.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.360
In addition to the many characters that influenced
Bill Finger and Bob Kane when creating Batman,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.950
the Dark Knight himself has a few in-universe
heroes who inspired his superhero career.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.170
The most well-known, of course, is Zorro.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.640
The legendary swordsman is fictional in Bruce
Wayne's world, but his black, caped costume

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.020
imprinted itself onto Bruce's mind when he
saw The Mark of Zorro on the night his parents

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.790
were killed.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:44.740
Another major figure who led Bruce to the
life of a vigilante was the original Green

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.520
Lantern, Alan Scott.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.031
This iteration of the Green Lantern not only
defended Gotham in the mid-20th century, but

00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:53.250
also met a young Thomas Wayne.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.270
Bruce also has a childhood connection to Alan,
having once witnessed the Green Lantern evade

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.170
several blasts from the original Icicle's
Cold Gun.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.000
Batman: The Animated Series introduces another
costumed role model for Bruce Wayne: the Gray

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.890
Ghost, a fictional TV hero played by Simon
Trent — whose voice actor happens to be

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.480
none other than Adam West.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.580
"As a kid, I used to watch you with my father.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.880
The Gray Ghost was my hero."

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.709
"So it wasn't all for nothing…"

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:25.280
Both the Gray Ghost and his alter ego are
eventually introduced in the main comics continuity,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.810
though not simultaneously: The Gray Ghost
debuts in comics with a different man under

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.810
the mask, named Clancy Johnson, while Simon
Trent makes his first in-continuity appearance

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.940
in Gotham Academy #4, during DC's "New 52"
timeline.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:45.120
A surprising debate has raged within the Batman
fandom in recent years: Does Batman kill?

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.150
Some might point to Batman v Superman: Dawn
of Justice, in which he clearly does kill

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.989
more than a few people; the same holds true
in the two Tim Burton-directed Batman movies.

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.440
In Christopher Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy,
Batman is more than happy to let his enemies

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.489
die, even if he refuses to kill them himself.

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:05.099
Hardcore Batman fans, however, will point
out that movies aren't exactly the source

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:09.220
material — and in the comics, the issue
is far more complicated.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.310
Batman certainly has no problem dispatching
his foes during his earliest appearances,

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:17.129
but he moves away from the practice not long
after Robin enters the picture.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:20.819
Those early killings are removed from the
"main" Batman's history when the Golden Age

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:23.540
Batman is retconned into having existed on
Earth-Two.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.590
Since then, Batman has been largely against
killing, once even calling murder "theft of

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:29.680
the highest order."

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.709
That said, he's been known to break this rule,
having been responsible for a few deaths in

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:38.970
certain runs — and he even tries to kill
Ra's Al Ghul in Batman Annual #8.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:43.110
He also may have unintentionally killed a
few dolphins armed with plastic explosives

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.319
in Detective Comics #405, although, for some
reason, this incident doesn't seem to come

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:51.290
up much during fans' arguments over the subject.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:56.489
Ask anyone what colors Batman's costumes tend
to be and they'll probably list off black,

00:03:56.489 --> 00:03:58.000
blue, gray, and yellow.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.190
What they might not know, however, is that
Batman's very first costume also included

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.430
a color more commonly associated with his
greatest enemy.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.930
That's right — in addition to being much
smaller than they'd later become, Batman's

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.210
gloves were once purple.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:15.190
Batman's small, purple gloves are a one-issue
wonder, appearing in his debut story in Detective

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.070
Comics #27.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:22.319
The Dark Knight would then leave his hands
bare in #28 and on the cover of #29, before

00:04:22.319 --> 00:04:26.500
very overtly deciding to wear blue gloves
on the pages of that same issue.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.889
Since then, his handwear has been mostly black,
gray and blue, but the purple gloves have

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:33.620
returned on a few notable occasions.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.280
These include Scott Snyder and Greg Capullo's
Zero Year storyline in the 2011 Batman comic

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.180
series, in which they form a part of a more
makeshift costume for Batman.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.990
They also showed up on the Golden Age version
of Batman in Detective Comics #1027 and the

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.330
two recent Generations one-shots.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:54.800
You might think the famous giant penny in
Batman's cave is a "memento" snagged from

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.650
Two-Face, given how coins are essential to
his decision-making process.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:03.789
"You live… you die."

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:05.800
"Now we're talking."

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:12.550
In some cases, you'd be right: In the post-Crisis
continuity, the massive copper coin is revealed

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.759
to be Two-Face's; it also belonged to him
in Batman: The Animated Series.

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:22.099
Originally, however, the man behind the mammoth
penny is the aptly-named Joe Coyne, who debuts

00:05:22.099 --> 00:05:24.719
in World's Finest Comics #30.

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:28.909
Years of frustration from constantly trying
to amass wealth and only ending up with pennies

00:05:28.909 --> 00:05:32.099
leads Coyne to "coin" himself the Penny Plunderer.

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:37.069
He swears revenge on the police who have incarcerated
him by dedicating himself to crime, with pennies

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.099
as his signature motif.

00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:42.740
Batman and Robin confront the Plunderer at
a museum while Coyne and his gang are trying

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:47.229
to steal an oversized 1 cent stamp — in
the process, they manage to subdue some of

00:05:47.229 --> 00:05:50.780
the thieves with a giant penny that happens
to be at the same exhibition.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.740
Not long thereafter, the penny ends up in
Batman's cave.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:57.249
Since then, pretty much every origin story
for the penny has either involved the Penny

00:05:57.249 --> 00:06:00.180
Plunderer, Two-Face, or even both characters.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.539
Interestingly, the same series that introduces
the penny, World's Finest Comics, later contradicts

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:10.309
its earlier origin for the penny by saying
Batman had obtained it while dueling the Joker.

00:06:10.309 --> 00:06:13.929
Maybe giant pennies just aren't that hard
to come by in Gotham City.

00:06:13.929 --> 00:06:18.820
Batman is no stranger to strange adventures,
and by the end of the 1940s the character

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.020
had encountered bizarre supervillains, time
travel, and even Martians.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:28.309
Yet when the 1950s and early '60s rolled around,
Batman began feeling a little more...

00:06:28.309 --> 00:06:29.309
super.

00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:33.419
During these stories, Batman's comic book
adventures became far more similar to Superman's,

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:37.529
as alien threats, sci-fi gadgets and monsters
crop up more often.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:42.500
Similarly, Batman's allies, who already included
Robin and occasionally Batwoman, expand into

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:47.740
a full-on Bat-Family — echoing how a Super-Family
formed around Superman during the same time

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.740
period.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.050
These growing similarities to Superman were
far from accidental.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:56.939
DC Comics management at the time pushed Batman
editor Jack Schiff into having the Caped Crusader's

00:06:56.939 --> 00:07:01.529
comics emulate the Man of Steel's, whose comics
outsold Batman's by quite a margin.

00:07:01.529 --> 00:07:06.259
Sadly, that sales gap didn't narrow when Batman
tried doing things Superman's way, prompting

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:10.279
the Dark Knight's adventures to revert to
a more successful "back-to-basics" approach

00:07:10.279 --> 00:07:12.900
in 1964.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.879
If you've ever looked at Batman's Zur-En-Arrh
costume and decided that nobody on Earth would

00:07:16.879 --> 00:07:21.169
ever willingly wear something like that — well,
you wouldn't be that far off from the truth.

00:07:21.169 --> 00:07:26.080
Originally, the suit belongs to an alien named
Tlano, who becomes the Batman of the planet

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.080
Zur-En-Arrh after viewing the original Batman's
exploits with long-range equipment.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.499
Tlano then enlists his role model's help in
fending off an invasion from another alien

00:07:34.499 --> 00:07:38.919
world, and Batman is surprised to discover
that simply being on Tlano's planet grants

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:41.949
him super-strength, invulnerability and flight.

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:43.270
Sound familiar?

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:49.569
Small wonder, as the story, from Batman #113,
is titled "Batman – Superman of Planet X!"

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:53.569
During writer Grant Morrison's run on Batman,
the Batman of Zur-En-Arrh is retconned into

00:07:53.569 --> 00:07:58.360
being Bruce Wayne himself, specifically an
alternate persona created to counter mind

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.360
control.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.680
Consequently, the events of Batman #113 are
explained as a hallucination induced by Simon

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.689
Hurt — and perhaps even a subconscious desire
on Batman's part to have powers like Superman.

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:14.459
This would also explain why the Zur-En-Arrh
costume is so colorful, although Batman overtly

00:08:14.459 --> 00:08:19.789
seems to attribute that to Robin in Batman
#680, and it's hard not to note the visual

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:21.659
similarities between the two.

00:08:21.659 --> 00:08:23.509
As for where the name "Zur-En-Arrh" comes
from?

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:27.559
A misremembered "Zorro in Arkham," which is
the end of a sentence Thomas Wayne utters

00:08:27.559 --> 00:08:32.589
after he, Martha, and Bruce have finished
watching The Mark of Zorro.

00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:36.229
One strange quirk of Bruce Wayne's life is
that crucially important events often seem

00:08:36.229 --> 00:08:39.360
to happen to him while he's attending masquerade
balls.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.039
One example is a costume party held in his
youth, at which his father wears a Bat-themed

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:48.550
disguise and is even referred to as a "Bat-Man"
when he wins the prize for best costume.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:53.129
Clearly, the get-up leaves an impact on Bruce,
as he himself admits in the same issue.

00:08:53.129 --> 00:08:57.980
Soon after, Batman even puts on the outfit
himself to scare mobster Lew Moxon.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.079
Simon Hurt also wears the costume as part
of his ploy to convince Batman that he is

00:09:02.079 --> 00:09:05.560
Thomas and somehow survived Joe Chill's attack.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:08.980
The other masquerade ball that leaves a mark
on Bruce is the one at which Barbara Gordon

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.360
debuts as Batgirl, in Detective Comics #359.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.910
One of the biggest scenes in the film Batman
Returns even takes place at a masquerade ball,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:22.180
in which Bruce Wayne and Selina Kyle learn
each other's costumed identities and the Penguin

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.800
abducts his former associate, Max Shreck.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.350
Then, in The Dark Knight Rises, a slightly
less extravagant masquerade event plays host

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.649
to another meeting between Bruce Wayne and
Selina.

00:09:31.649 --> 00:09:39.209
"You're all gonna wonder how you could live
so large and leave so little for the rest

00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:40.209
of us…"

00:09:40.209 --> 00:09:44.399
Most of Batman's partners in crime-fighting
are pretty conventional, as far as sidekicks

00:09:44.399 --> 00:09:45.399
go.

00:09:45.399 --> 00:09:48.360
Naturally, there are the many Robins who have
worked for him over the years, some of whom

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.070
have graduated to new identities, such as
Nightwing.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:56.550
There have also been multiple Batgirls, along
with unique junior heroes such as Bluebird.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:58.800
There are some pretty strange outliers, however.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.509
One such example is Jarro, a tiny leftover
piece of Starro the Conqueror that Batman

00:10:03.509 --> 00:10:06.910
takes in as his own and places in a jar in
the Hall of Justice.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:11.589
The diminutive starfish views Batman as a
father and dreams of becoming his next crime-fighting

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:15.899
partner, even donning a small Robin costume
at one point and holding his own against the

00:10:15.899 --> 00:10:17.180
Legion of Doom.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.910
He also happens to be the strongest psychic
the DC Universe has ever known.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:25.180
Batman's sidekicks get even odder once you
venture outside of continuity: The Bizarro

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:30.019
World graphic novel, for instance, introduces
Monkey the Monkey Wonder, a circus monkey

00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:34.560
Batman adopts and tries to induct into the
crime-fighting life... to less than stellar

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.250
results.

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:41.029
DC's heroes have often been compared to mythological
characters, with Batman being frequently equated

00:10:41.029 --> 00:10:42.930
to Hades or Pluto.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:47.560
So it's perhaps natural that he has particularly
unique interactions with Darkseid, the New

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:49.730
God of the fiery planet Apokolips.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:54.720
In Superman/Batman #12, for instance, Batman
earns the god's respect by threatening to

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.689
create hundreds of new fire pits across the
planet unless Darkseid releases his hold on

00:10:59.689 --> 00:11:00.689
Supergirl.

00:11:00.689 --> 00:11:05.350
The two face off again during Final Crisis,
when Batman fires a bullet at him made of

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:10.410
Radion; Darkseid retaliates by sending Batman
back in time with his Omega Beams, simultaneously

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:13.990
turning the Dark Knight into a kind of "bomb"
against time itself.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:18.899
There have been times where Batman has also
served Darkseid, albeit not always loyally.

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:23.029
When the Justice League accidentally hands
Earth to Darkseid on a platter by destroying

00:11:23.029 --> 00:11:28.019
the Philosopher's Stone in the JLA story "Rock
of Ages," Batman gains the New God's trust

00:11:28.019 --> 00:11:31.620
by posing as the infamous Apokoliptian torturer
Desaad.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:36.240
This storyline likely influenced Batman's
role in Justice League Dark: Apokolips War,

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.180
which is set in a future where Darkseid's
also conquered Earth and the Dark Knight serves

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.290
him willingly, having been brainwashed.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:47.370
Rather than disguising himself as one of Darkseid's
traditional servants, however, Batman performs

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:52.639
his duties as himself, albeit connected to
Metron's Mobius Chair — a nod to another

00:11:52.639 --> 00:11:55.639
comic book saga, The Darkseid War.

00:11:55.639 --> 00:11:59.639
Bruce Wayne officially begins his crusade
as Batman as an early adult, meaning he never

00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:01.980
spent any time as a teenage superhero.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:08.100
Yet in the Elseworlds story Titans: Scissors,
Paper, Stone, a teenager named Jamadagni Renuka

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:13.579
attaches a device to her ex-boyfriend Alec
Swanwick's neck, downloading Batman's personality

00:12:13.579 --> 00:12:14.660
into his mind.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:19.149
While he's never outright confirmed to be
the original Dark Knight, the mentally transformed

00:12:19.149 --> 00:12:23.499
Alec's demeanor, gruff voice and deductive
skills make it pretty clear he's meant to

00:12:23.499 --> 00:12:24.629
be the Batman himself.

00:12:24.629 --> 00:12:29.129
In this story, he's given a different codename
— "Captain Thug" — while Jamadagni and

00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:34.060
her friends are christened names like "Witchy-Poo"
and "Prosthetic Lass," which are subtle references

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.570
to Teen Titans Raven and Cyborg.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:41.509
That said, in the main DCU canon, multiple
Teen Titans have taken on the Batman mantle.

00:12:41.509 --> 00:12:46.290
During "Battle for the Cowl," ex-Titans Jason
Todd and Tim Drake briefly assume the Batman

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.519
identity.

00:12:47.519 --> 00:12:53.290
Dick Grayson, the Titans' original leader,
has also been Batman, in both the 1990s "Prodigal"

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:57.800
storyline and after Darkseid sends Bruce Wayne
back in time.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:03.190
Many readers of DC Comics during the 1990s
may remember Elseworlds, a DC Comics sub-imprint

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:06.730
meant to reinterpret DC's characters in various
ways.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:10.880
Throughout that decade and into the early
2000s, Elseworlds stories were a staple of

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:15.899
the DC line, with several of the imprint's
stories being incorporated into the DC equivalent

00:13:15.899 --> 00:13:16.999
of the multiverse.

00:13:16.999 --> 00:13:21.980
The funny thing is that Elseworlds was essentially
created by accident, according to writer-editor

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:26.670
extraordinaire Brian Augustyn, who wrote the
very first issue without realizing it at the

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.160
time: Batman: Gotham by Gaslight.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.199
As he explains in the deluxe edition of the
graphic novel, Gotham by Gaslight first came

00:13:33.199 --> 00:13:38.499
about when fellow comics giant Mark Waid suggested
an annual of the Secret Origins title that

00:13:38.499 --> 00:13:42.930
told alternative histories of DC favorites
— essentially the germ of what Elseworlds

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:44.089
would become.

00:13:44.089 --> 00:13:49.279
Gotham by Gaslight, however, became a standalone
story instead, and years of Elseworlds specials,

00:13:49.279 --> 00:13:52.430
one-shots and graphic novels followed in the
same vein.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.491
Interestingly, the first comic to actually
be given the title Elseworlds was not Gotham

00:13:56.491 --> 00:14:02.209
by Gaslight, but another Batman story — namely
Batman: Holy Terror, written by Secret Origins

00:14:02.209 --> 00:14:05.209
contributor Alan Brennert.

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:08.671
Several big-name actors have come close to
assuming the role of the Caped Crusader at

00:14:08.671 --> 00:14:11.819
one point or another, from Pierce Brosnan
to Bill Murray.

00:14:11.819 --> 00:14:15.689
Interestingly, many of the performers who
almost became Batman still ended up appearing

00:14:15.689 --> 00:14:18.639
in superhero movies — primarily as villains.

00:14:18.639 --> 00:14:22.389
Josh Brolin was considered for the role of
the Dark Knight in Batman v Superman: Dawn

00:14:22.389 --> 00:14:27.610
of Justice, but instead wound up taking his
MCU role as Thanos to great heights; he also

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:31.199
became a major Marvel Comics hero, Cable,
in Deadpool 2.

00:14:31.199 --> 00:14:35.089
Jake Gyllenhaal also auditioned for the role
of Batman in Christopher Nolan's films and

00:14:35.089 --> 00:14:39.550
eventually joined the MCU as Mysterio in Spider-Man:
Far From Home.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:43.540
Some actors who almost played Batman even
landed a villain role in the very same film.

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:47.779
Cillian Murphy auditioned for the role of
Batman in Batman Begins and was later cast

00:14:47.779 --> 00:14:49.410
as Scarecrow instead.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:50.879
"He's here."

00:14:50.879 --> 00:14:51.879
"Who?"

00:14:51.879 --> 00:14:53.079
"The Batman…"

00:14:53.079 --> 00:14:58.750
Meanwhile, Heath Ledger, who played the Joker
in The Dark Knight, declined to play Batman

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:02.249
in Begins after being offered the part personally
by Nolan.

00:15:02.249 --> 00:15:05.740
Amazingly, Ledger's reason for doing so was
that he claimed he would never take a part

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:07.980
in a superhero film.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:12.040
In addition to inspiring cosplayers around
the world to don his cowl, Gotham's Creature

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:16.839
of the Night has imitators who have gone as
far as to adapt his superhero lifestyle, too.

00:15:16.839 --> 00:15:21.939
Canada has Brampton Batman, aka Stephen Lawrence,
who spends his evenings helping those in need

00:15:21.939 --> 00:15:24.079
and even stopping the occasional crime.

00:15:24.079 --> 00:15:29.350
"If you'd like, I can hold you like this."

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:33.450
There's also Alexander Brovedani, alias Toronto
Batman.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.899
Though largely retired, he still makes an
occasional public appearance.

00:15:36.899 --> 00:15:41.480
Japan, meanwhile, has "Chibatman," the Batman-like
hero of the city Chiba.

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:45.949
He once told the BBC his goal is to "bring
the smiles back" to everyone he meets, ever

00:15:45.949 --> 00:15:48.730
since the 2011 Great East Japan Earthquake.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:52.569
Dark Knights have also been spotted around
the U.S. as well, the most endearing perhaps

00:15:52.569 --> 00:15:58.139
being Batkid, whose famous one day-turn as
the Caped Crusader is the subject of the documentary

00:15:58.139 --> 00:15:59.399
Batkid Begins.

00:15:59.399 --> 00:16:03.819
Sadly, not every Batman has shared Bruce Wayne's
long career: the Batman of the U.S. state

00:16:03.819 --> 00:16:08.490
of Maryland, Lenny B. Robinson died during
a traffic accident in 2015.

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:12.720
Nevertheless, he left behind an admirable
legacy of brightening children's days whenever

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.829
he'd set foot in hospitals dressed as Gotham
City's most famous defender.

00:16:17.829 --> 00:16:21.750
Not every government official in Gotham City
likes Batman, but he's got himself at least

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:27.459
one major real life politician on his side:
U.S. Senator Patrick Leahy of Vermont.

00:16:27.459 --> 00:16:31.411
Leahy has been in nearly every Batman movie
from Batman Forever through the Dark Knight

00:16:31.411 --> 00:16:36.269
trilogy and up to Batman v Superman, only
skipping Batman Begins along the way.

00:16:36.269 --> 00:16:40.740
He's even lent a voice to Batman: The Animated
Series, on which he played the Governor.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:45.209
Leahy sat out The Batman, however — as much
as he'd have liked to take part, he explained

00:16:45.209 --> 00:16:49.610
that his workload as Senator kept him from
making his customary trips to Gotham for Matt

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:50.779
Reeves' movie.

00:16:50.779 --> 00:16:54.970
In addition to joining Batman on the silver
screen, Leahy has added his own dab of ink

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:56.610
to Batman's comic book adventures.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:01.540
Both Detective Comics: 80 Years of Batman
and Batman: Death of Innocents: The Horror

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:05.069
of Landmines bear text pieces written by the
Senator.

00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:09.140
As Leahy told The Washington Post, the latter
comic — which the Vermont politician gave

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:13.010
to the entire Senate — may have actually
been instrumental in getting his historic

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:15.159
anti-landmine legislation passed.

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:18.399
